  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Sunday...\n\nConvection continues this morning bringing weak showers and \nthunderstorms producing wind gusts to 30 mph and lightning. \nActivity should diminish through the overnight hours before \nincreasing again later this morning.\n\nRidging continues across the Northern Rockies this week. High \ntemperatures will regularly be in the 90s to 100F each day along \nwith daily afternoon shower and thunderstorm chances.\n\nToday, a few of the latest HiRes model runs are depicting \nthunderstorms developing near and east of the Pryors and northern \nBighorn mountains later this morning. The strength of these storms\nis worth monitoring, as the latest HRRR and RRFS show severe wind\ngusts (greater than 60 mph) in southern Powder River and \nprogressing through Carter County into western South Dakota. A \nMarginal Risk for strong to severe thunderstorms has been \nintroduced across southern Montana and northern Wyoming for severe\nwind gusts in excess of 65 mph today.\n\nSmoke from western wildfires will continue to spread across the \narea today as well. Along with some reductions to visibility, \ndaytime high temperatures may be a bit lower and minimum relative \nhumidities may remain a touch higher. Increasing cloud cover will \nalso contribute to potentially lower highs and higher minimum \nrelative humidities.\n\nSeveral neural networks are depicting an increasing chance for \nstrong to severe thunderstorms over eastern Montana, Tuesday \nthrough Thursday. Continue to monitor the forecast as this week \nprogresses.\n\nModels continue to depict a potential pattern change next week, \nwith a system out of the northwest flattening the pattern and \nbringing westerly flow.\n\nMatos",
